{% sec 1, "The First Hedge Fund", "Who invented the hedge fund, and what was the idea?", "The first hedge fund" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Imagine betting on which runners in a race will beat the others, without caring whether it's a fast race or a slow one. You back the runners you think are strong and bet against the ones you think are weak. If a headwind slows everyone down, your "for" bets lose a bit and your "against" bets gain a bit; what's left is how good you were at picking runners.</p>
    <p>That was Alfred Winslow Jones's idea in 1949. Jones was a sociologist and financial journalist writing for <em>Fortune</em> magazine, not a Wall Street insider. He bought shares he thought were cheap and {% gl "short selling" %}sold short{% endgl %} shares he thought were expensive. A fall in the whole market hurt his longs but helped his shorts, so his returns came mainly from his stock picking. He called it a "hedged fund", and the name stuck.</p>
    <p>His fund had four features that every hedge fund since has borrowed:</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>Leverage.<span class="why">Because the shorts cut the market risk, Jones felt safe borrowing to make his best ideas bigger.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>A performance fee.<span class="why">He took \(20\%\) of the profits and no flat fee at all, so he only got paid when his investors made money. He reportedly took the idea from Phoenician merchants, who kept a fifth of the profit from each voyage.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>A private partnership.<span class="why">Set up for a small number of wealthy investors, it sat outside the 1940 law that limits mutual funds (the last note explains how).</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>His own money in.<span class="why">Jones put almost everything he had into the fund alongside his investors: "eating your own cooking".</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>It worked. From 1955 to 1965 his fund returned \(670\%\), against \(358\%\) for the best-performing mutual fund. Almost nobody knew, until a <em>Fortune</em> article in 1966 revealed the record. Within two years, about \(140\) hedge funds were open.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Boom, Bust and the Missing Hedge", "What went wrong for the first wave of copycats?", "The first boom and bust" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The new managers copied Jones's partnership, his \(20\%\) fee and his leverage. Most dropped the one thing that made it a <em>hedge</em> fund: the shorts. In the roaring late-1960s market, shorting looked like a waste. They borrowed to buy the fashionable growth companies of the day, the {% gl "Nifty Fifty" %}"Nifty Fifty"{% endgl %} such as Xerox, Polaroid, Avon and IBM, which traded at \(50\) to \(80\) times earnings on the belief their growth would never slow.</p>
    <p>Then the market turned. In the 1969–70 bear market the S&amp;P 500 fell about \(36\%\) from peak to bottom, and borrowed, all-long portfolios were crushed; many funds that had boasted spectacular returns in 1967–68 were gone within eighteen months. The 1973–74 crash, driven by the oil embargo, high inflation and the collapse of the Nifty Fifty, finished the job: the S&amp;P 500 fell nearly \(50\%\), and the number of hedge funds shrank from about \(150\) to fewer than \(70\). The survivors, Jones among them, were mostly the ones that had kept hedging and kept their borrowing modest.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
