  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The pattern to remember</span>
    <p>In bull markets, managers drop their risk discipline and borrow more, because it pays. When the market turns, the borrowing that multiplied the gains multiplies the losses, and there's no hedge to cushion them. Discipline dropped in greed, rediscovered in crisis: it's the story of every hedge fund boom since.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "The Macro Era", "Who brought hedge funds back in the 1980s and 1990s?", "The macro era" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The revival came from a new style: {% gl "global macro" %}global macro{% endgl %}, betting on whole economies through currencies, interest rates, bonds and stock indices. It suited the times. The fixed exchange rates of the Bretton Woods system had broken down in the early 1970s, so currencies now floated and could move a lot. Big moves meant big opportunities.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>George Soros</strong> ran the Quantum Fund from 1973, mixing economic analysis with his theory of {% gl "reflexivity" %}reflexivity{% endgl %}: prices don't just reflect the world, they change it, which can push markets far from fair value before they snap back. His most famous trade came on 16 September 1992, Black Wednesday. Britain was trying to hold the pound inside Europe's exchange rate system; Soros bet roughly \(\$10\) billion that it couldn't. The UK gave up and the pound fell, earning his fund a reported \(\$1\) billion or more almost overnight.</li>
      <li><strong>Julian Robertson</strong> started Tiger Management in 1980 with \(\$8\) million and grew it to about \(\$22\) billion with deep research: long the best companies, short the worst. Tiger closed in 2000 after Robertson refused to chase the dot-com bubble. His bigger legacy is his people: the {% gl "Tiger Cub" %}"Tiger Cubs"{% endgl %}, analysts who left to start their own funds, including Chase Coleman (Tiger Global), Andreas Halvorsen (Viking Global) and Philippe Laffont (Coatue). Together they manage hundreds of billions.</li>
      <li><strong>Michael Steinhardt</strong> and <strong>Paul Tudor Jones</strong> were the era's other stars. Tudor Jones became famous for making money in the October 1987 crash.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The era ended with a warning. In 1998 Long-Term Capital Management, run by star bond traders with two Nobel-winning economists, lost \(\$4.6\) billion in weeks on about \(25\)-to-\(1\) leverage, and the New York Fed organised a \(\$3.6\) billion rescue by \(14\) banks. The Crashes That Changed the Rules note has the outline; the LTCM note in Famous Funds and Blow-ups tells it in full.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "The Institutions Arrive", "How did hedge funds go from a club for the rich to a trillion-dollar industry?", "The institutions arrive" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Until the 1990s, hedge fund money came mostly from wealthy individuals. Then David Swensen, who ran Yale University's endowment, showed a different way to run a big pot of money. Instead of mainly owning shares and bonds, the {% gl "Yale Model" %}Yale Model{% endgl %} put a large share into "alternatives": hedge funds, private equity, property and natural resources. Yale's returns topped \(13\%\) a year over two decades, and other endowments, pension funds and sovereign wealth funds rushed to copy it. Big institutions went from almost nothing in hedge funds in the mid-1990s to \(5\) to \(20\%\) of their assets by the mid-2000s.</p>
    <p>Industry assets grew from about \(\$500\) billion in 2000 to \(\$1.9\) trillion by 2007, and three things changed with the new money:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>{% gl "fund of funds" %}Funds of funds{% endgl %}</strong> boomed. They pool money from smaller investors and spread it across many hedge funds, doing the research and checks a small pension couldn't do itself. At their peak they controlled about \(40\%\) of all hedge fund money.</li>
      <li><strong>Funds had to grow up.</strong> Institutions demanded proper operations, compliance, technology and detailed reporting. Ten people in an office with a spreadsheet no longer passed the checks.</li>
      <li><strong>Strategies multiplied.</strong> There was too much money for long/short equity and macro alone, and institutions wanted returns that didn't move with the stock market. Credit, event-driven, distressed debt, convertible arbitrage and statistical arbitrage funds all became categories of their own. The Strategies chapter covers each one.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
